  "rationale": "The spec is area-dominated (min luts_plus_ffs) with a hard throughput floor of >=1 MSPS and a tight accuracy bound of max_abs_err <= 2^-10. Accuracy depends only on data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding, so I bound data_width at 12 (Q1.10 output, just meets 2^-10) up to 16 for margin, and n_iter at 10..16 (10 CORDIC iterations give ~2^-10+ accuracy; more only adds area). angle_guard 0..2 and frac_guard 0..2 keep the angle/atan and datapath widths lean while preserving accuracy. For throughput, only the 1-result/cycle families (pipelined, pipelined_m) reliably meet >=1 MSPS at realistic Fmax, so they get 80% of the budget; pipelined_m is the star because m>1 cuts FFs versus full pipelining while keeping 1/cycle throughput, directly serving the area objective. unrolled_k gets 20% as the potential area champion, but its throughput scales as Fmax/k so it is a conditional candidate. iterative is excluded: at 1 result/(N+3) cycles it cannot plausibly reach 1 MSPS without an unrealistic Fmax. This split focuses the 400-eval budget on the families that can satisfy the constraints and exposes the area-vs-Fmax trade-off that defines the Pareto front."
