{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "The current Pareto front is dominated by pipelined_m designs (7 of 8 points) and one unrolled_k design. The `iterative` family has not been explored yet. Given the objective is to minimize `luts_plus_ffs` (area) while meeting a throughput constraint of >= 1 MSPS and an accuracy constraint of <= 2^-10, the `iterative` family is a strong candidate for the low-area end of the front. It uses a shared datapath with barrel shifters and only 1 micro-rotation per cycle. While its throughput is lower (1 result every N+3 cycles), if N is small (e.g., 10-14) and Fmax is high, it can easily meet the 1 MSPS constraint (e.g., N=14 -> 17 cycles/result. If Fmax is ~60 MHz, throughput is ~3.5 MSPS). Crucially, `iterative` typically has significantly lower LUT/FF counts than pipelined architectures because it does not require deep pipeline registers or wide parallel datapaths for multiple stages. The current best area is 503 (unrolled_k k=5). `iterative` with similar accuracy (N~14, W~15) might achieve an even lower area (potentially < 400) while still meeting the throughput constraint, thus expanding the hypervolume towards the low-area corner. Exploring this family is necessary to ensure we have mapped the true feasible Pareto front for the 'low_area_control' spec.",
